     outputs/metrics.json, which the pipeline writes; edit the template, not this file. -->

Between $ch_from and $ch_to, $ch_dec Speed Zones features in Victoria had their posted limit lowered. This
project asks a narrow, answerable question with open data only:

> On road segments whose limit was **lowered** between mid-2023 and end-2024, did police-reported **injury
> crashes** change, compared with similar segments whose limit did **not** change?

It is an observational evaluation. It can say how crashes moved on treated roads relative to comparable
roads. It cannot prove the limit change caused that movement.

## Key results

| | Rate ratio after vs before (95% CI) |
|---|---|
| Naive before–after, treated roads only | $naive_rr ($naive_lo–$naive_hi) |
| Trend on comparison roads | $trend_rr ($trend_lo–$trend_hi) |
| **Comparison-group difference-in-differences (main)** | **$did_rr ($did_lo–$did_hi)** |
| Same DiD, fatal + serious injury crashes only | $fsi_rr ($fsi_lo–$fsi_hi) |
| Empirical Bayes before–after (fails its placebo, see below) | $eb_rr ($eb_lo–$eb_hi) |
| Placebo: same DiD with the change moved 3 years earlier | $pl_did_rr ($pl_did_lo–$pl_did_hi) |

- **Sample.** $treated_units treated road segments ($treated_km km, $treated_schemes change requests)
  versus $comparison_units never-changed segments. $did_units treated segments ($did_km km) fall in strata that
  inform the estimate. They had $did_tb injury crashes in the 36 months before the change and $did_ta afterwards
  ($after_min–$after_max months, to 2025-12).
- **Main estimate.** Injury crashes on treated roads were **$did_phrase** than the comparison-road trend
  predicts. The rate ratio is $did_rr, with a 95% CI of $did_lo–$did_hi ($did_range). The evidence is modest:
  in $sens_cross1 of $sens_n sensitivity analyses the interval reaches 1 (no change). Point estimates
  range from $sens_min to $sens_max.
- **By type of change.** The clearest reductions are on roads lowered from 60 km/h and from 90–110 km/h,
  but each subset alone is inconclusive. Local streets lowered from 50 to 40 km/h show no clear change
  over this short follow-up.
- **Checks.** The placebo (fake change three years earlier) returns $pl_did_rr, so treated and comparison
  roads moved together before the change. Varying the before window from 12 to 60 months keeps the
  estimate between $bw_min and $bw_max (main analysis, 36 months: $did_rr). If regression to the mean were
  driving the result, short windows would show bigger reductions.
- **Why the comparison group matters.** In the placebo period, a naive before–after count says crashes on
  these roads rose $pl_naive_pct% ($pl_naive_rr). The rise came from the post-lockdown rebound, which
  comparison roads also show ($pl_trend_rr). The DiD correctly reports no change.
- **Empirical Bayes fails its own placebo** ($pl_eb_rr, $pl_eb_lo–$pl_eb_hi, with no real change). Its
  safety performance function has no current traffic volumes (open AADT stops in 2019), so it cannot tell a
  busy road from an unlucky one and over-corrects. The main estimate is therefore not based on EB.
- **Yarra 30 km/h trial (case study).** Injury crashes on the trial streets went from $y_tb in $y_mb months
  to $y_ta in $y_ma months. The DiD rate ratio is $y_did_rr ($y_did_lo–$y_did_hi, p = $y_did_p). The study is
  underpowered: it would detect a true 20% reduction only $y_pow20% of the time.

